WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.230
So, there used to be this thing called a party.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:07.110
The concept was simple—gather a bunch of
living, breathing bodies in the same place

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.300
at the same time and just see what happens.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.180
Some parties were promises—and those were
called “weddings,” and other parties were

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.840
goodbyes—and those were called “funerals.”

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.650
There were parties to “warm” new homes
and parties to mark the day you were born

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.440
and parties to signal the arrival of a new
year.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.520
And if my friends wanted me to be on time
to a party, they’d lie—

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.499
I wasn’t proud of my reputation with time
but…

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.040
SINDHA: Do you think my lateness is genetic?

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.680
DAD: I don’t know.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.680
SISTER: No.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.140
MOM: Yeah because it’s too much like your
dad’s.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.940
There’s a lot of different ways to be late
but you’re late in this particular way that’s

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.280
exactly like him.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.440
SINDHA: Have you ever heard of IST?

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:47.400
SISTER: Yeah.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.720
Indian Standard Time.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.620
DAD: It means…

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.340
SISTER: It means that you don’t have a sense
of time.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.900
*laughs*

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.140
Once someone was describing their color blindness
to me and it reminded me a lot of how I feel

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.460
about time.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.220
How I know that 3pm and 3:05 are technically
different, but I personally don’t perceive

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.400
that contrast.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.840
Time resembles color in other ways, too — we
can only access the smallest sliver of both

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:13.850
spectrums.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.680
Non-linearity and relativity remind me of
ultraviolet and red-green — what scientists

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.539
call the “Impossible Colors,” colors we
can measure, but can’t actually see.

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:26.590
And like color, time is continuous.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.469
We can’t locate the seam of an hour, the
border of a day — the same way we can’t

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:34.399
declare with any precision where yellow becomes
orange or orange becomes red.

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:40.630
And yet, over the course of human evolution
we’ve insatiably sought to structure time.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:45.299
Dividing the sun into angles and tidily organizing
the story of our lives into years.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:50.830
It kind of embarrassed me — humans taking
the unfathomable expanse of time and refining

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:51.830
it into hours.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:57.740
The universe is 13.8 billion years old — who
are we to assert the importance of a minute?

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.979
But I tried my best to banish those thoughts.

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:03.850
I bought clocks and set them to five minutes
early.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.619
I filled calendars with meticulous, month-long
plans.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:10.580
I even put a whiteboard on my fridge and every
morning, I wrote the date down.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.830
I was finally closing the gap — becoming
one of those people who holds the reins of

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.220
time.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.060
And then —

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.680
The pandemic happened.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.380
And suddenly Tuesdays were Thursdays were
Sundays.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.640
Flowers bloomed and wilted and babies learned
to crawl and grey hairs grew in, but the larger

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.070
story of time felt interrupted — bland birthdays
and cancelled weddings and solitary holidays.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.760
Doing the same thing in the same place with
the same people, day after day after day.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.420
Previously imperceptible shades of time showed
up at our doorsteps.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.700
Like special relativity — in the spring,
time moved glacially as it does alongside

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.950
black holes.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.710
And then, without warning, it accelerated
— so that when I asked my mom what day in

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.680
May we were on, she gently informed me it
was July.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.220
Or non-linearity.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.120
How we emerged after months spent holed up
in our homes, only to find ourselves reliving

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.460
the very same moment that had driven us inside
in the first place.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.790
The whole world joined me in temporal disorientation
— even my punctual superiors were at a loss.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:15.890
They knew how to arrive five minutes early&nbsp;
— not how to repeat the same five minutes

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.660
43,854 times.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.560
I regretted taking time for granted.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.000
Now I would give anything to hear someone
say, “the party starts at five, the doors

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.480
close at eight — don’t be late.”

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.760
It turns out our perception of time is incredibly
malleable — even color can distort it.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.340
When people are shown blue and red stimuli
of the same duration — they consistently

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.790
overestimate the blue and underestimate the
red.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:47.220
Temperature also warps time — the hotter
we are, the faster we feel it passing.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.610
And music, too.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.530
Oddly, uptempo music decelerates time and
downtempo music hastens it.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.350
So, if smooth jazz, heat waves and a bit of
blue are enough to mangle time, no wonder

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.580
a pandemic upended it.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.720
In 1983, a paper published in the journal
Science described an experiment in which researchers

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.320
claimed to have overridden the human eye’s
opponency mechanism — allowing people to

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.560
see “impossible colors.”

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.630
The participants said the colors were vivid
and awe-inducing — but entirely indescribable.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.990
Like seeing red for the first time and having
no name for it.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:27.290
I imagine them returning to their lives, tucking
the impossible colors away, into the closets

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.190
where we store our most inarticulable memories.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:35.320
But had they not been alone in what they’d
witnessed, had the whole world woken up one

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.860
day, suddenly able to see a new color — I
think we’d have created a name in a matter

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.240
of hours.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.750
Because when it comes to color, we innately
gravitate towards classifying what we see.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.820
Naming the shade between orange and red “pink,”
calling the blue of the Aegean “royal,”

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.020
and the blue of the Caribbean “aquamarine.”

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.730
But when it comes to time, we have such a
limited lexicon.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:56.560
Fast.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.300
Slow.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:57.800
Long.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.300
Short.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:04:58.860
Future.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.440
Present.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.020
Past.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.960
Beyond that — we’re pretty much speechless.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:03.900
But not hopeless.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.740
In 1812, Dietrich Nikolaus Winkel invented
the metronome.

00:05:09.751 --> 00:05:13.790
But two hundred plus years later, classical
composers still prefer to communicate musical

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.600
time in sentimental Italian.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.600
Tempo allegro — cheerful time.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.920
Tempo allegro ma non troppo — cheerful time
but not too much.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.240
Tempo rubato — stolen time.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.330
Maybe we’ve been too fixated on fixing our
metronomes when what we need most is vocabulary

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.980
for these new colors of time.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.080
To describe 17-second months, millennium-long
days and a year without any parties.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.889
Language for when time undergoes a phase transition
right there in your hand — days melting

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:50.150
and months evaporating and years freezing.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.980
Maybe there’s a word for that in Italian.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.040
Maybe it translates to “impossible time.”

